### What are the actors and core use cases?

The actors are the rider (requests trips, pays), the driver (goes online, accepts trips, drives, gets paid), and admin/operations (monitors, intervenes). The core flows define the services: driver goes online and streams location, rider requests a ride, the system matches a nearby driver, the trip runs with live tracking, the fare is computed and charged, and both parties are notified and rated.

```java
package com.bank.ridehailing.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TripRequest {
    private String riderId;
    private double pickupLat;
    private double pickupLng;
    private double dropLat;
    private double dropLng;
    private BigDecimal estimatedFare;

    public TripRequest(String riderId, double pickupLat, double pickupLng,
                       double dropLat, double dropLng) {
        this.riderId = riderId;
        this.pickupLat = pickupLat;
        this.pickupLng = pickupLng;
        this.dropLat = dropLat;
        this.dropLng = dropLng;
    }

    // Getters omitted for brevity
    public String getRiderId() { return riderId; }
    public double getPickupLat() { return pickupLat; }
    public double getPickupLng() { return pickupLng; }
}
```

### How do you decompose into microservices?

Each service owns its data following database-per-service. The Location service uses a geo-store optimized for spatial queries. The Payment service uses a strongly consistent relational database with idempotency.

| Service | Responsibility | Database Type |
|---|---|---|
| User/Auth | Profiles, tokens | Relational (PostgreSQL) |
| Location | GPS stream, current positions | In-memory geo (Redis Geo) |
| Trip | Trip lifecycle | Relational (PostgreSQL) |
| Matching | Nearest driver, dispatch | Reads from Location's geo-store |
| Pricing | Fare + surge calculation | Relational + cache |
| Payment | Charge rider, pay driver | Relational (strong consistency) |
| Notification | Push updates | Queue-backed |
| Ratings | Feedback | Relational |

```java
package com.bank.ridehailing.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class PaymentService {

    public PaymentResult chargeRider(String riderId, String tripId,
                                      BigDecimal fare, String idempotencyKey) {
        // Idempotency: if this key was already processed, return stored result
        // Outbox pattern: write payment + event in one transaction
        return new PaymentResult(tripId, "SUCCESS", idempotencyKey);
    }
}
```

### How does real-time location work?

Drivers send GPS coordinates every few seconds. This is a very high write rate of small updates. The location service consumes pings through a message stream and keeps each driver's current position in a fast in-memory store with a geospatial index. Only the latest position per driver is kept for matching, while raw trails go to cheaper storage for analytics.

```java
package com.bank.ridehailing.location;

import redis.clients.jedis.Jedis;
import redis.clients.jedis.GeoCoordinate;

public class DriverLocationStore {

    private final Jedis jedis;
    private static final String GEO_KEY = "drivers:active";

    public DriverLocationStore(Jedis jedis) {
        this.jedis = jedis;
    }

    public void updateLocation(String driverId, double lat, double lng) {
        jedis.geoadd(GEO_KEY, lng, lat, driverId);
    }

    public java.util.List<String> findNearby(double lat, double lng,
                                              double radiusKm) {
        return jedis.georadiusByMember(GEO_KEY, "pickup-point", radiusKm,
            redis.clients.jedis.params.geo.GeoRadiusParam.geoRadiusParam()
                .sortAscending().count(10))
            .stream()
            .map(r -> r.getMemberByString())
            .collect(java.util.stream.Collectors.toList());
    }
}
```

