# Driver D102 at 28.6150, 77.2100
# findNearby(28.6145, 77.2095, 2.0) returns [D102, D101]
```

### How does the matching engine work?

When a rider requests a trip, the matching service queries the geo-index for candidate drivers near the pickup, filters by availability and vehicle type, ranks by estimated time of arrival, and sends a trip offer. If the driver declines or times out, it offers the next. Crucially, it claims the driver atomically so two concurrent requests cannot grab the same driver.

```java
package com.bank.ridehailing.matching;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;

public class MatchingEngine {

    private final DriverLocationStore locationStore;
    private final DriverAvailabilityStore availabilityStore;

    public MatchingEngine(DriverLocationStore locationStore,
                          DriverAvailabilityStore availabilityStore) {
        this.locationStore = locationStore;
        this.availabilityStore = availabilityStore;
    }

    public Optional<String> findAndClaimDriver(double pickupLat, double pickupLng) {
        List<String> candidates = locationStore.findNearby(pickupLat, pickupLng, 5.0);
        for (String driverId : candidates) {
            // Atomic claim: compare-and-set from AVAILABLE to ASSIGNED
            boolean claimed = availabilityStore.tryClaimDriver(driverId);
            if (claimed) {
                return Optional.of(driverId);
            }
        }
        return Optional.empty();
    }
}
```

### How do you design the database layer?

The trip database is relational, storing trips with status, rider, driver, pickup, drop, timestamps, and fare. The payment database is relational and strongly consistent. The location "database" is the in-memory geo-store for current positions plus a time-series store for historical trails.

